chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 18 of the 3rd edition, CSV, JSON, and XML Files, teaches three plain-text data formats: CSV with the csv module (reader, writer, tabs instead of commas, DictReader and DictWriter) and Project 13 that removes the header row from every CSV file of a folder; JSON with json.loads() and json.dumps(); XML with xml.etree.ElementTree (fromstring, parse, the children of an element, iter, Element, SubElement, set, tostring) and the third-party xmltodict; and it ends with 11 practice questions and one practice program, the Excel-to-CSV converter. The chapter page of 2026-10-12 is 83,216 bytes with 35 listings (20 of them interactive sessions of 97 statements), 1 captioned figure (Figure 18-1) and no table, and its sha256, c998127be7342ac419f9f884a60aea6ef837e3dba1c2deada1e977a6a20a01dc, equals the chapterDigest of the book's own chapter ontology [P01] [P03] [P04]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The online materials"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The online materials of the 3rd edition (7,759,877 bytes, 91 files) hold exampleWithHeader3.csv, example3.xlsx, removeCsvHeader.zip (100 CSV files), excelSpreadsheets.zip (26 CSV files) and the Excel files produceSales3.xlsx and censuspopdata.xlsx, but no example3.csv, no my_data.xml, no output.csv and no JSON or XML file at all, although the chapter's listings open example3.csv and my_data.xml; a reader has to type them. The Practice Program tells the reader to unzip excelSpreadsheets.zip and test an Excel-to-CSV converter on it, but that zip holds 26 files that are already CSV (spreadsheet-A.csv to spreadsheet-Z.csv) and no .xlsx file, so a converter that skips non-xlsx files writes nothing from it. The shipped exampleWithHeader3.csv has CRLF line ends and the year 2025 in its timestamps where the chapter prints 2035, and it has 7 data rows where the first list printed in the chapter has 6 dictionaries [P01] [P02]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The replay of the chapter"@en ; sen0414:findingText "All 97 interactive statements of the chapter were replayed in the order of the page under Python 3.14.6. As printed, 89 matched. The two xml_string listings fail as typed: their closing quotation mark of the xsi namespace is a curly quotation mark, so ET.fromstring() and xmltodict.parse() raise a not well-formed error and the name root is then undefined; json_string is wrapped over four lines inside its quotes, which is a SyntaxError. With the curly quotation mark replaced 93 statements matched; the 4 that still differ are the year (2025 in the shipped file against 2035 printed, 2 statements) and the loop that prints None for address and phone, which prints a line of whitespace instead because the statement before it, tree = ET.parse('my_data.xml'), rebinds root to the pretty-printed file, whose address and phone elements hold the whitespace between their tags. With that statement left out 92 of 94 matched. The same result came with the Appendix A pins (xmltodict 0.13.0, openpyxl 3.1.5) [P01] [P02] [P05]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P05 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Appendix B and the chapter text disagree"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Appendix B answers question 3, what modes the File objects for reader and writer objects need to be opened in, with read-binary (rb) for Reader objects and write-binary (wb) for Writer objects, but the chapter writes open('output.csv', 'w', newline='') and open('example3.csv') in text mode, and under Python 3.14.6 csv.reader on a file opened in rb raises Error: iterator should return strings, not bytes, and csv.writer on a file opened in wb raises TypeError: a bytes-like object is required, not str. Answer 1 begins In Excel, spreadsheets can have values of data types other than strings, which is correct for question 1. The chapter also says XML syntax is similar to HTML, which we covered in Chapter 18, but Chapter 18 is this chapter and HTML is the subject of Chapter 13 (Web Scraping), which the chapter itself names elsewhere [P01] [P06]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Line numbers are not record numbers"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Project 13 skips the header row with a test of reader.line_num, keeping a row only if line_num is not 1. The Python documentation says line_num is the number of lines read from the source iterator and is not the same as the number of records, because records can span several lines. With a header cell that holds a line break (a quoted field Name and (first)), the header record ends at line_num 2, the test keeps 3 of 3 records, and the header is still there; next(reader, None) skips the header record whatever its length. On the 100 shipped files, which have one-line headers and no embedded line breaks, the program as written is right: every output equals the input without its first record (19,631 records in, 19,531 out) [P01] [P07]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "A for loop and memory"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says looping over the reader object, instead of calling list() on it, is the way to save memory with a big file. On a generated file of 300,000 rows (7.5 MB) the peak memory measured with tracemalloc was 69.0 MiB for list(reader) and 0.166 MiB for a for loop, a ratio of 416, and the loop took 0.40 s against 0.90 s; a DictReader loop took 1.22 s. The claim holds, and a reader object has no len() and no index and can be read once: a second list() on it returned 0 rows [P01] [P07] [P23]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P23 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The encoding the chapter never mentions"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Every listing of the chapter calls open() without an encoding, and the chapter does not mention encodings. In text mode the encoding is then the locale encoding, which is UTF-8 here and is cp1252 for most Windows users in the United States. A file of UTF-8 text read as cp1252 gives mojibake without an error (Ad,Sehir becomes a mixture of Ã and Å characters) and a file saved as cp1254 and read as UTF-8 raises UnicodeDecodeError. An Excel file saved as CSV UTF-8 starts with a byte order mark, which csv.reader returns as part of the first field and which DictReader makes part of the first key (\\ufeffName); encoding='utf-8-sig' removes it. Python 3.15 makes UTF-8 mode the default (PEP 686): under 3.15.0b4 sys.flags.utf8_mode was 1 where 3.11 to 3.14.6 gave 0, and python.org lists 3.15.0 as released on 9 October 2026 although uv offered only 3.15.0b4 [P15] [P16] [P24] [P26] [P62] [P64]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P15, chx:P16, chx:P24, chx:P26, chx:P62, chx:P64 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Line ends and the newline argument"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says that on Windows a reader must pass newline='' to open() or the rows of output.csv will be double-spaced (Figure 18-1) and that the reason is beyond the book. The reason: csv.writer ends every record with CRLF, and a text file opened without newline translates every line feed to the platform line end, so on Windows CRLF becomes CRCRLF. Reproduced by asking io.TextIOWrapper for the translation Windows does: the bytes were a,b CR CR LF, csv.reader returned each record followed by an empty record, and LibreOffice 24.2 opened the file with the layout of Figure 18-1 (rows 1, 3 and 5 filled, 2 and 4 empty). The Python documentation says a file object given to csv.reader or csv.writer should be opened with newline='' and that it should always be safe to specify it, since the csv module does its own universal newline handling. The figure's alt text, Screenshot of a Windows computer desktop showing six overlapping calculator programs, does not describe it: the picture is a spreadsheet [P01] [P07] [P15] [P59]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P15, chx:P59 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "What csv offers that the chapter leaves out"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter does not mention the quoting constants (QUOTE_MINIMAL, QUOTE_ALL, QUOTE_NONNUMERIC, QUOTE_NONE and, since Python 3.12, QUOTE_STRINGS and QUOTE_NOTNULL), the limit of 131,072 characters per field (a field of 131,073 raises Error), skipinitialspace, strict mode, escapechar, the Sniffer, the dialects excel, excel-tab and unix, restkey and restval of DictReader, the ValueError of DictWriter for a key not in fieldnames, and the fact that the writer turns None into an empty field and True into True and so cannot return a type. QUOTE_STRINGS and QUOTE_NOTNULL do not exist in Python 3.11 and exist in 3.12 to 3.15; a read with QUOTE_NOTNULL gives None for an unquoted empty field and an empty string for a quoted one [P07] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P07, chx:P19 .
chx:F10 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "JSON values and what the chapter leaves out"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter lists the six JSON value types and tells the reader that trailing commas, single quotes and comments are not allowed. All of it holds under Python 3.14.6 (json.loads of ['spam', 'eggs',] raises Illegal trailing comma before end of array at column 16), but the message differs by release: Expecting value in 3.11 and 3.12, the trailing-comma message from 3.13. The chapter does not say that a tuple becomes a list (and does not come back), that integer, float, None and True keys become strings, that a set, datetime, Decimal, bytes or complex raises TypeError, that Python accepts NaN, Infinity and -Infinity, which RFC 8259 does not allow, that 5,000 digits exceed the 4,300-digit limit for converting a string to int, that json.load and json.dump read and write files, that ensure_ascii turns non-ASCII text into escapes, or that the Python documentation still names RFC 7159, which RFC 8259 obsoleted in December 2017, as the specification [P01] [P08] [P29] [P31]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P08, chx:P29, chx:P31 .
chx:F11 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "JSON in Python 3.14 and 3.15"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.14 adds notes to the TypeError of a serialization error that name the path to the value (when serializing list item 1, when serializing dict item a) and allows python -m json, now preferred to python -m json.tool; under 3.11 to 3.13 the notes are absent and python -m json exits with an error. Python 3.15 adds the array_hook parameter to load() and loads() and, with csv, deprecates the __version__ attribute of the module: under 3.15.0b4 reading json.__version__ or csv.__version__ raised a DeprecationWarning that says removal is slated for Python 3.20 [P17] [P64]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P17, chx:P64 .
chx:F12 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "ElementTree reads text as strings and None, and has only a subset of XPath"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says the text of a self-closing element is None (true, root[3].text is None for car) and shows None for address and phone, which holds for the compact string and not for a pretty-printed file; it says XPath can locate elements, but the module has a limited subset and no xpath() method (lxml has one); every text is a str (age is the string 30, programmer is the string true); an element's truth value is False for an element without children and, since Python 3.12, testing it emits a DeprecationWarning, so find() results must be tested with is not None. iter() returns the root first and then every descendant in document order (16 elements for the chapter's person, 6 children). ET.tostring() writes a self-closing element with a space before the slash (<car xsi:nil='true' xmlns:xsi='...' />), where the chapter prints it without; with the default encoding it returns bytes and non-ASCII text becomes character references; encoding='unicode' returns a str; ET.indent() (3.9) pretty-prints in place [P01] [P09] [P17] [P19]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P09, chx:P17, chx:P19 .
chx:F13 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Namespaces and xsi:nil"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter writes the car element with xsi:nil='true' and a hand-set xmlns:xsi attribute. ElementTree stores a namespaced attribute under its full name, {http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance}nil, drops xmlns:xsi from the attributes, and never turns nil into None: an element with xsi:nil='true' and text keeps its text, as the XML Schema specification says such an element must be empty for the document to be valid, which ElementTree does not check. xmltodict keeps @xsi:nil and @xmlns:xsi as keys of a dict, so car is a dict and not None; ET.tostring() writes an attribute named xsi:nil with no check that the prefix is declared, and the parser refuses the result with unbound prefix [P01] [P09] [P37] [P38]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P09, chx:P37, chx:P38 .
chx:F14 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "XML security, and a chapter that never says it"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter parses XML with ET.fromstring() and xmltodict.parse() and never says that XML from an untrusted source can be an attack. A 760-byte billion laughs document that would expand to 3,000,000,000 characters is refused by ElementTree under Python 3.14.6 (Expat 2.8.1) with limit on input amplification factor (from DTD and entities) breached, in 0.12 s; the Python documentation says Expat versions lower than 2.7.2 may be vulnerable, and 3.14.0 to 3.14.5 carry Expat 2.6.3 and 3.11 to 3.13 carry 2.6.1. An external entity (file:///etc/hostname) was not read (ElementTree reported undefined entity), defusedxml 0.7.1 raised EntitiesForbidden for all three documents, xmltodict 1.0.4 refuses every entity (ValueError: entities are disabled) and xmltodict 0.13.0, the version Appendix A pins, parsed the billion laughs file and the small entity. The billion laughs attack is catalogued as CWE-776 and external entities as CWE-611 [P09] [P10] [P13] [P40] [P43] [P49] [P54] [P55] [P56]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P09, chx:P10, chx:P13, chx:P40, chx:P43, chx:P49, chx:P54, chx:P55, chx:P56 .
chx:F15 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The Excel-to-CSV practice program"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The printed skeleton is not a program: its for loop has no indented body (IndentationError) and it uses wb, sheet and csv_file without defining them. A converter written to it and run on example3.xlsx, produceSales3.xlsx and censuspopdata.xlsx exited 0 and wrote 5 CSV files in 4.3 s; the dates came out as 2035-04-05 13:34:02, not as the 4/5/2035 13:34 the chapter prints for Excel, because openpyxl returns datetime objects and csv.writer writes their str(); LibreOffice 24.2 writing the same file with its CSV filter wrote 04/05/2035 13:34:02. The skeleton evaluates sheet.max_column inside the row loop, and each evaluation visits every cell of the sheet: on produceSales3.xlsx (23,758 rows, 4 columns) the first 2,000 rows took 5.94 s with the limit inside the loop and 0.006 s with it hoisted, so the nested loop as printed costs time proportional to rows times cells. Sheet2 and Sheet3 of example3.xlsx are empty and gave a file holding one empty quoted field [P01] [P02] [P41] [P42] [P59]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P41, chx:P42, chx:P59 .
chx:F16 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Formats compared, and the versions"@en ; sen0414:findingText "On the chapter's Alice data the compact JSON is 248 characters, indent=2 makes it 326, and the XML without whitespace is 407 (1.64 times the JSON) and 563 as printed. JSON keeps int, bool and null across a round trip; XML through ElementTree or xmltodict and CSV give strings only; CSV needs a flat row and cannot hold the nested address and phones; tomllib (3.11) reads TOML and has no dumps and TOML has no null; shelve is binary and Python-only. Across 11 interpreters (3.11, 3.12, 3.13, 3.14.0 to 3.14.6, 3.15.0b4) 50 items were compared and 11 differ: the quoting constants (3.11), the empty quotechar message (3.14), the trailing-comma messages (3.13), the exception notes and python -m json (3.14), the iterparse close() method (3.13), the truth-value DeprecationWarning (3.12), the bundled Expat (2.6.1, 2.6.3, 2.8.1), the default encoding mode (3.15) and the __version__ DeprecationWarning (3.15). The newest releases on PyPI on the day were xmltodict 1.0.4 (22 February 2026), openpyxl 3.1.5 (28 June 2024), defusedxml 0.7.1 (8 March 2021, a 0.8.0rc2 exists) and lxml 6.1.3 [P08] [P14] [P25] [P40] [P42] [P43] [P44] [P45]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P08, chx:P14, chx:P25, chx:P40, chx:P42, chx:P43, chx:P44, chx:P45 .
chx:F17 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Where the three formats come from"@en ; sen0414:findingText "CSV was in use on IBM mainframes in 1972 and was written down in RFC 4180 in October 2005, which says records end in CRLF, the last record may or may not end in a line break and a header line is optional [P28] [P46]. JSON was specified by Douglas Crockford, sent as a first message in April 2001, described by RFC 4627 in 2006, standardized as ECMA-404 in 2013 and as RFC 8259 in 2017 [P29] [P30] [P33] [P47]. XML 1.0 was published by the W3C on 10 February 1998 and its fifth edition is dated 26 November 2008 [P35] [P36] [P48]. ElementTree entered the standard library with Python 2.5 on 19 September 2006 and json with Python 2.6 on 1 October 2008 [P57] [P58]. The chapter says nothing about any of this, which is why the six stories of the course supply it." ; sen0414:cites chx:P28, chx:P29, chx:P30, chx:P33, chx:P35, chx:P36, chx:P46, chx:P47, chx:P48, chx:P57, chx:P58 .
chx:F18 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "The XML statements of the chapter, tested"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter says whitespace outside the opening and closing tags is insignificant, but ElementTree reports it: in a pretty-printed file the text of an element that holds only elements is the whitespace between the tags (address had a line break and eight spaces) and the tail of an element is the whitespace after it. It says tag and attribute names can be written in any case, which holds only if the end-tag matches the start-tag: <A></a> raised a mismatched tag error and <Person><NAME>x</NAME></Person> parsed. It calls a document with several roots not valid and says valid documents must have a single root; the W3C word for a document that obeys the syntax is well-formed, and valid means compliance with a document type declaration, which ElementTree never checks (a person with a banana child parsed, and a DTD checked with lxml refused it). It says attribute values can be in single or double quotes (true) and does not say that a value with no quotes, an attribute with no value, an unescaped & or < and an HTML entity such as &nbsp; are all errors, which they are [P01] [P09] [P35] [P36]." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P09, chx:P35, chx:P36 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "33 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "64 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-10-12T12:00:00; 47 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
